WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.113
Suppose three workers store sales records and
their local maximum orders are eighty, one

00:00:05.113 --> 00:00:10.284
hundred twenty, and ninety dollars. To find the
global maximum, each worker scans its own

00:00:10.284 --> 00:00:15.630
records and sends just one candidate. The
coordinator compares three numbers and returns
one

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:16.501
hundred twenty.

00:00:16.501 --> 00:00:21.443
Shipping every original order to the coordinator
would do far more network work without

00:00:21.443 --> 00:00:26.612
improving this result. The key is an algebraic
property: the maximum of local maxima equals

00:00:26.612 --> 00:00:31.611
the maximum of the union. Draw computation
beside each partition and only the candidates

00:00:31.611 --> 00:00:32.804
crossing the network.

00:00:33.001 --> 00:00:37.586
This reasoning explains why some analyses
parallelize easily while others require

00:00:37.586 --> 00:00:42.171
substantially richer intermediate information or
several rounds of communication.

00:00:42.171 --> 00:00:47.411
This table shows why averaging averages can go
wrong. Partition A contains one order worth

00:00:47.411 --> 00:00:52.593
ten dollars. Partition B contains nine orders
with a total value of nine hundred dollars.

00:00:52.593 --> 00:00:57.542
Their local means are ten and one hundred. But
the two partitions represent different

00:00:57.542 --> 00:00:58.590
numbers of orders.

00:00:58.671 --> 00:01:03.768
If we give those two means equal weight, we get
fifty-five. That answer treats a one-order

00:01:03.768 --> 00:01:08.808
partition as equally important as a nine-order
partition. The arithmetic is easy, but the

00:01:08.808 --> 00:01:09.884
weighting is wrong.

00:01:09.884 --> 00:01:15.292
Instead, send a sum and a count from each
partition. Add the sums to get nine hundred ten.

00:01:15.292 --> 00:01:20.520
Add the counts to get ten. Divide to obtain the
correct mean of ninety-one. The general

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:25.988
lesson is to derive the information your summary
must preserve. Local medians, for example,

00:01:25.988 --> 00:01:28.693
do not generally determine the global median.

00:01:28.884 --> 00:01:33.980
Count words in two documents: the first says red
blue red, and the second says blue green.

00:01:33.980 --> 00:01:39.189
Each mapper emits a word and one for each
occurrence. The shuffle groups all values with
the

00:01:39.189 --> 00:01:42.246
same word, regardless of which document produced
them.

00:01:42.384 --> 00:01:47.614
Reducers then add the grouped values, producing
red two, blue two, and green one. Draw the

00:01:47.614 --> 00:01:52.960
blue emissions from both documents converging on
the same group. This is the important step:

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:58.132
the programmer's chosen intermediate key tells
the framework which information must meet.

00:01:58.132 --> 00:02:03.022
The framework handles distribution and grouping,
while the mapper and reducer define the

00:02:03.022 --> 00:02:08.023
calculation. An incorrect key can produce an
efficient parallel job that answers the wrong

00:02:08.023 --> 00:02:08.524
question.

00:02:08.632 --> 00:02:13.811
A batch job has a finite input and eventually
completes, but its tasks may execute on many

00:02:13.811 --> 00:02:19.047
machines at once. Scheduling a mapper near its
input reduces the need to move the raw data.

00:02:19.047 --> 00:02:23.995
The shuffle can still be expensive because
records with matching keys must meet across

00:02:23.995 --> 00:02:24.628
partitions.

00:02:24.632 --> 00:02:29.688
If one word dominates the corpus, the
corresponding reducer may become a bottleneck
even

00:02:29.688 --> 00:02:34.628
when the input is evenly divided. Chaining jobs
creates more intermediate datasets and

00:02:34.628 --> 00:02:39.913
communication stages, so sketch those
intermediates before assuming that more machines
solve

00:02:39.913 --> 00:02:40.603
the problem.

00:02:40.632 --> 00:02:45.480
Retries also matter: a failed task may run
again, which is safe for framework-managed

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:50.386
results under its contract but needs additional
care for external side effects such as

00:02:50.386 --> 00:02:51.355
sending messages.

00:02:51.355 --> 00:02:57.542
A dashboard showing orders per minute cannot
wait for the entire order stream to finish,

00:02:57.542 --> 00:03:03.517
because new orders keep arriving. It defines a
window and produces an answer for that

00:03:03.517 --> 00:03:09.844
bounded interval while processing continues. Now
let an order happen at 12:00:58 but reach

00:03:09.844 --> 00:03:13.218
the processor at 12:01:04 after a network delay.

00:03:13.355 --> 00:03:18.520
Event time places it in the earlier minute;
arrival time places it in the later one. Choose

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:23.685
which meaning the dashboard requires, then
define whether a previously emitted count can be

00:03:23.685 --> 00:03:27.771
corrected. Calling the system real time does not
answer those questions.

00:03:27.855 --> 00:03:33.115
The architecture needs explicit rules for window
boundaries, late data, and the freshness

00:03:33.115 --> 00:03:34.947
the user can reasonably expect.

00:03:34.947 --> 00:03:40.083
A stream counter remembers partial results
between messages. If it crashes after adding an

00:03:40.083 --> 00:03:45.162
order but before recording its input position, a
restart may read that order again. If it

00:03:45.162 --> 00:03:50.127
records the position first and loses the counter
update, it may skip the order instead.

00:03:50.127 --> 00:03:55.245
Recovery must coordinate the state with the
consumed position under a defined processing

00:03:55.245 --> 00:04:00.479
contract. Checkpointing and replay are common
building blocks, while external effects need

00:04:00.479 --> 00:04:05.655
their own idempotency or transaction design. A
fast operator can also accumulate lag when

00:04:05.655 --> 00:04:10.947
arrivals exceed its capacity, so low latency is
an operating condition rather than a label.

00:04:11.127 --> 00:04:15.987
Compare a nightly batch report and a
continuously updated dashboard on their required

00:04:15.987 --> 00:04:20.275
freshness and recovery costs, not on whether
either can process large data.
